</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present UniCom, a unified multimodal model via compressed continuous semantic representations. We adopt the Transfusion architecture (Pathway I in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Two Ways of Predicting Representations ‣ 3 UniCom ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) as our default design, which is validated to outperform the Query-Guided alternative in <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
We describe the implementation details in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Implementation Details ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and report performance on reconstruction, text-to-image generation, and image editing benchmarks in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. In <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we ablate the key design choices of our compressed representation, including the optimal feature shape and projector (compressor and decompressor) architecture.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our unified model is built upon Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024b)</a></cite> for multimodal understanding and FLUX.1-dev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs (2024)</a></cite> for high-fidelity image generation, utilizing SigLIP2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tschannen et al. (2025)</a></cite> as the semantic encoder.
To effectively bridge the modality gap, we employ a multi-head self-attention compressor for feature compression and a two-layer MLP for visual projection.
The training pipeline consists of four progressive stages: alignment, pre-training, continued training, and supervised fine-tuning, all adopting a native-resolution strategy.
Please refer to <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation details ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for comprehensive details regarding the decoder architecture, hyperparameter settings, data mixtures, and the ablation setup.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion Decoder.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the quantitative evaluation on the ImageNet validation set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2009)</a></cite> in Tab <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Notably, comparing the uncompressed (d1152) and compressed (d64) variants, we observe that reducing the channel dimension by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="18\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18\times</annotation></semantics></math> incurs negligible loss in reconstruction fidelity.
The qualitative superiority of our approach is further evidenced in Fig <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Diffusion Decoder. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ UniCom: Unified Multimodal Modeling via Compressed Continuous Semantic Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. As shown in the zoomed-in regions, our method achieves reconstruction fidelity comparable to the specialized Flux VAE, effectively recovering high-frequency details such as small text characters and maintaining consistent facial identity. This stands in sharp contrast to prior methods relying on semantic features, which often exhibit severe degradation in these fine-grained structures. Consistently with recent findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al. (2026)</a></cite>, we observe that incorporating high-quality text-rich data during training is instrumental in enhancing text rendering performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10702v1/editing_cases_v2_comp.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/549;" width="476" height="549" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Comparison of the results for image editing, highlighting UniCom performance in tasks such as image manipulation, object swapping, and color adjustment. See more visualization in the Appendix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10702v1/diffusion_decoder.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of the proposed diffusion decoder and reconstruction analysis.</span>
Given an input image, the vision encoder extracts semantic features which are then processed by compressor and decompressor to condition the diffusion model for image reconstruction.
During training, the compression modules and the diffusion backbone are optimized, while the encoder remains frozen.
We investigate the bottleneck capacity by varying the token number <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and feature dimension <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
As illustrated in the bottom row, compressing the dimension <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> yields superior reconstruction fidelity, whereas reducing the token number <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> leads to noticeable blurring in fine details.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10702v1/comparison_recon_v4_compressed.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/265;" width="476" height="265" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual comparison of image reconstruction results</span>.
Notably, our method (d64) maintains high fidelity in high-frequency details (e.g., text characters) and preserves facial identity better than these semantic-based baselines, achieving quality comparable to the specialized Flux.1-dev VAE.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
